WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.550
We've separated interface from implementation for services,

2
00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:09.580
for policies, and for tests.

3
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:10.450
Last stop:

4
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:11.210
messaging.

5
00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:15.280
Your code shouldn't know whether an event rides MQTT,

6
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.200
Kafka, Azure

7
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.440
— or never leaves the JVM.

8
00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.060
This is the entire contract a service sees:

9
00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.890
publish, asyncPublish, publishToOperation

10
00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.340
— and messageArrived.

11
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.320
No broker types anywhere.

12
00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:31.540
publishToOperation targets a service operation,

13
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:36.120
resolved through the same service registry that carries its policies.

14
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.640
Messaging is a first-class entry point

15
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.280
— not a side channel.

16
00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:42.090
One interface.

17
00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:43.830
Four implementations:

18
00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.150
chenile-mqtt, chenile-kafka,

19
00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:49.630
chenile-azure, and chenile-jvm-pub-sub.

20
00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.020
Run the publisher on the in-JVM bus:

21
00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.010
no broker, messages flow in-process.

22
00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.860
Now swap the dependency to chenile-kafka,

23
00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.820
and run the same code against a real broker.

24
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.620
Not one line of business code changed.

25
00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:08.110
Here's why it matters beyond portability.

26
00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:11.230
An inbound message becomes a ChenileExchange,

27
00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:15.910
and runs through the same interception pipeline as an HTTP call.

28
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.610
So security, tenancy,

29
00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.200
i18n and logging apply to events automatically.

30
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.810
You never write a second authorization path.

31
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.160
With the cloud-edge switch,

32
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:31.130
the same messages route between edge devices and a cloud broker

33
00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:32.710
— originated at the edge,

34
00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.480
handled centrally, with identical logic.

35
00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.690
Same philosophy as everything else:

36
00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:40.450
depend on the interface,

37
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.890
and choose the implementation by configuration.

38
00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:48.090
Chenile is an in-VM message bus that also speaks HTTP.

39
00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:49.670
Next episode:

40
00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.720
configuration management, with cconfig.
